[gd_scene load_steps=3 format=3 uid="uid://bgubmaprange01"]

; Highsun Grounds — the seventh map, and the first one that is not an arena
; (D-113). The practice range: a cleared bog under a high sun with a lodge at
; the south end, three throwing lanes, a sixty-metre bow lane, a gallery, a
; melee pit, an ability yard, a parkour course and void off the north and east
; edges. It was Glowworm Grounds while it was a bog at night, and the name went
; up with the sun.
;
; Like Kopje Crossing, Lantern Wharf, Halcyon Wake and Twin Quarry this scene
; has no geometry in it at all. `scripts/world/maps/range_map.gd` extends
; `StaticMap` and builds the whole place in `_ready()` from its layout tables,
; then calls `super()`, which sweeps it into world-space trimesh collision on
; layer 1, and then adds the dressing: ten torches, the MegaKit reed and snag,
; and the ambience. There were twenty-four glowworm lanterns as well — unshaded
; emissive spheres on the tops of the lane posts — and they are gone with the
; posts and with the fences the posts stood on.
;
; It also builds every `Marker3D` on the map in code rather than here, which is
; a departure from the other five and is deliberate. This map carries fifty-odd
; markers across eight groups, and every one of their positions is *derived* —
; a dummy stands on a distance mark its own lane's table gives, a board stands
; `BOARD_OFFSET` off the lane centre those four dividing lines give. Typed
; into a scene file those would be fifty coordinates that go stale silently the
; first time a lane moves. Built from the same tables as the geometry, they
; cannot.
;
; So this file is what `static_map.gd` asks for, and no more:
;
;   Environment  a lit day over the bog, at a fog density you can shoot through
;                (`range_env.tres`). See that file for what each knob did at
;                dusk and where it went when the sun came up.
;   Sun          **thirty-two degrees up and north-north-east** — thirty degrees
;                east of north — at 2.25 energy in a near-white. The sky's
;                disc follows this node (`sun_follow_light` in `range_sky.tres`),
;                so what you can see in the sky and where the shadows point can
;                never disagree. Serialised ROW-major, as every `.tscn` stores a
;                `Transform3D`: the nine floats are the basis's three **rows**,
;                not its columns, and the axes are the columns. Written as
;                columns this node once came out 59.6 degrees from where it was
;                meant to be — and the error was silent, because the moon it
;                replaced was due north, where the matrix is symmetric and the
;                transpose is itself. The light's own -Z — where the light
;                *goes* — is (-0.424, -0.530, +0.734), so the sun sits at
;                (+0.424, +0.530, -0.734): 32.0 degrees of elevation on a
;                bearing 30.0 degrees east of north.
;                `range_map._check_sun_and_sky` prints both back out of the
;                built scene and the gate reads them, because this is exactly
;                the kind of number that is wrong in a way no render obviously
;                shows.
;
;                It was an 11-degree moon due north, then a 9-degree sun on this
;                bearing, and it is now 32 degrees on the same bearing because
;                the owner asked for the sun higher in the air. **The bearing did
;                not move with it**, and the section below is why: it is the
;                number with the argument under it.
;
;                **2.25, and it was 3.0 and 4.2 before that.** Elevation did most
;                of the work when the sun came up, not energy: a key lands
;                sin(elevation) of itself on a horizontal surface and this whole
;                map is horizontal surface, so nine degrees put 15.6% of 4.2 down
;                (0.66) and thirty-two puts 53.0% of 3.0 down (1.59) — 2.4 times
;                the key on the peat off a lamp a third smaller. 3.0 was then too
;                much of a good thing: the owner played it and said *"the suns
;                lights is too strong and too powerful … the whole scene reads
;                orange"* (D-169). It is 2.25 now — a quarter off — and 53.0% of
;                that is 1.19 on the peat. What replaces the missing 0.40 is the
;                `Clouds` fill below and a sixth more sky ambient, which is the
;                half of the complaint that is about colour rather than about
;                strength: the same light on the ground, less of it arriving warm
;                and from one direction. The colour came off the amber with the
;                energy: (1, 0.91, 0.78) is a low sun seen through six airmasses,
;                and a sun thirty-two degrees up is barely reddened at all, so it
;                is (1, 0.945, 0.88). Kopje Crossing's noon is 1.3 at 64 degrees
;                (1.17 on the flat) over pale sand; this is wet peat at 0.21
;                albedo, so 1.19 off a floor a third as reflective is the same
;                picture, not a darker one — and the top-down render agrees, with
;                the open peat's median at 59.8 of 255 against the 58.5 a 3.0 sun
;                left it at.
;   Clouds       a second, shadowless directional light from the south-south-west
;                — the same bearing the old `Bounce` came from, swung from six
;                degrees **under** the horizon to twenty-four **over** it, in a
;                near-white with a purple-blue cast at 0.68. It was a bounce off
;                the peat and it is light off the cloud deck. See the node.
;
;   Spawns       filled by the script: eight pads on the lodge deck, two rows of
;                four, all facing north up the range. The deck is 24 m wide
;                because `preview_map.PAD_SEPARATION` is 6 m and eight pads have
;                to fit on it.
;   Bases        built by the script at (-20, 0, 30) and (20, 0, 30), symmetric
;                about x = 0 so `CaptureLayout` splits the deck's eight pads four
;                and four rather than by a handful of centimetres (D-051).
;   Letters      B in the bow lane, O out in the ability yard, G between the
;                gallery and the pit.
;
; `void_height` is -12: the ground simply stops at the north and east lips and a
; fall from it takes 0.86 s, which is a lesson rather than a wait. The melee pit
; floor at -1.5 has ten and a half metres of clearance under it, so nobody dies
; by walking into the melee ring.
;
; ## Thirty-two degrees, and thirty degrees east of north
;
; Both numbers are the renders' doing. The first one is the owner's — *"the sun
; needs to be higher in the air"* — and the second one is the one with an
; argument under it, which is why it did not move when the first one did.
;
; **Elevation.** This map has been at eleven degrees of moon, then nine of sun,
; and is now at thirty-two. The number that matters is sin(elevation), because
; that is the fraction of a key that lands on a horizontal surface and a range
; is nothing but horizontal surface: 11 degrees put 19% down, 9 put 15.6%, and
; 32 puts **53.0%**. Three and a half times the light on the peat, at two thirds
; of the lamp — see the `Sun` note above for the energy arithmetic. What it
; costs is the picture the low sun bought: the glory sits high instead of down
; the lane, the cumulus are lit from above rather than along their flanks, and
; the shadows are a sixth of the length they were. What it buys is a range you
; can read at sixty metres at any hour of the match, which is the thing a range
; is for.
;
; **Azimuth, and this is the load-bearing one.** A shadow from a 32-degree light
; is **1.60 times as long as the thing casting it** (1 / tan 32; at nine degrees
; it was 6.31). The west bank is 8 m of peat running the full 90 m length of the
; map, so it throws a **12.8 m** shadow, and where that lands is still a question
; of the bearing:
;
;   north-north-west  (the first attempt, D-113) — shadow laid *across* the map,
;                     which at nine degrees put two thirds of the range in
;                     permanent darkness. At 32 it is a tenth of it, and it is
;                     still the wrong way to lay a shadow on a place you shoot
;                     along.
;   due north         (what shipped once) — the bank shadows along its own length
;                     and therefore shadows nothing. It also puts the disc at the
;                     vanishing point of every lane, which is a sun in the
;                     crosshair of an archer at the firing line, and raising the
;                     sun does not fix that: it only moves the disc up the same
;                     line.
;   north-north-east  (here) — shadows run **south-south-west**, on (-0.5, +0.866).
;                     The bank's 12.8 m goes 6.4 m west and 11.1 m south: west is
;                     *off the map* (the bank is already the west edge) and south
;                     runs out under the lodge. It shadows nothing, and it does it
;                     while standing 30 degrees off every lane's axis.
;
; Everything else follows: the lodge ridge at 7.4 m throws 11.8 m, 10.3 m of it
; southward onto its own apron; the 3 m yard ledge throws 4.8 m into open yard;
; a 1.25 m cover block throws 2.0 m, which is a shape on the peat beside it and
; the reason a block reads as a block from twenty metres. The 2.2 m distance
; posts and the 0.60 m lane rails used to be in this list and are not on the map
; any more — see `range_map._build_lanes`.
;
; And the crosshair, which is better than it was. The lanes run due north; the
; sun is 30 degrees east of that and 32 degrees up. From a firing line with a
; level crosshair the disc sits 30 degrees right and **35.8 degrees up the
; frame** against a half-vertical FOV of 37.5 (the game's 75-degree vertical
; FOV), so it is in the top-right corner of the picture and out of it altogether
; the moment an archer lifts to loft a shot. At nine degrees it was 10.4 degrees
; up — in frame, ahead-right, and a lot nearer the sight.
;
; The cost, stated, and it is the same trade D-113 made: the faces a shooter
; sees are the *south-south-west* ones, which are the shaded ones. That is the
; right way round for a range — a target read as a shape against a bright sky is
; a target you can lead at forty-five metres — and the ambient is what keeps its
; near face from being a hole. A 32-degree sun makes that cheaper than it was:
; the tops are lit properly now, so a dummy is a lit crown over a shaded body
; rather than a silhouette.
;
; `tools/preview_map.gd` stands a Bog on every pad, `tools/parkour_report.gd`
; walks the jump graph and proves the course and the off-limits tops, and
; `tools/playthrough.gd -- range` runs the practice branch and plans the capture
; layout off the declared bases.

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://scripts/world/maps/range_map.gd" id="1_range_map"]
[ext_resource type="Environment" path="res://resources/config/range_env.tres" id="2_range_env"]

[node name="Range" type="Node3D"]
script = ExtResource("1_range_map")
void_height = -12.0
base_radius = 4.0

[node name="Environment" type="WorldEnvironment" parent="."]
environment = ExtResource("2_range_env")

[node name="Sun" type="DirectionalLight3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(-0.866025, -0.26496, 0.424024, 0, 0.848048, 0.529919, -0.5, 0.458924, -0.734431, 0, 40, 0)
light_color = Color(1, 0.945, 0.88, 1)
light_energy = 2.25
light_specular = 0.3
light_volumetric_fog_energy = 0.25
shadow_enabled = true
shadow_normal_bias = 1.6
directional_shadow_mode = 1
directional_shadow_max_distance = 120.0
directional_shadow_blend_splits = true

; The fill, and it is now **the thing the owner asked for by name** (D-169):
; *"there is a purple blue ish coming from the clouds to counter the orange …
; it should be gentle and mostly white, but it should be as if the sun light is
; coming off the clouds in that cooler color."* It was called `Bounce` for two
; passes because that is what it modelled — light off ninety metres of wet peat,
; arriving from *under* the horizon behind you, warm because peat is warm. That
; is a real thing and it was the wrong thing to lean on here: a warm fill under
; a warm key is why the whole scene read orange, and a bounce off brown ground
; can only ever make more brown.
;
; The job it does has not changed, and it is the reason a fill exists at all.
; The key reaches exactly two kinds of surface at any elevation: the tops of
; things and their north-north-east faces. Everything else on the map — every
; south face a shooter at a firing line is looking at, the deck the eight pads
; stand on under the lodge roof, the inside of the melee pit, the shaded half of
; every 1.25 m block — is lit by fill alone, and at nine degrees the first
; eye-height renders came back with eleven to sixteen per cent of the two back
; pads' frames at literal black. Kopje Crossing made the same argument under a
; *noon* sun over pale sand (D-061). The answer is still a shadowless
; directional almost anti-parallel to the Sun at a fraction of its energy; what
; changed is **where it comes from and what colour it is**.
;
; **Twenty-four degrees up, not six down.** Same bearing — south-south-west,
; 210 degrees, dead opposite the key, which is what puts it on every face the
; key misses — swung 30 degrees up through the horizon. A cloud deck is
; overhead, so light off one arrives from above; and because this light casts no
; shadow, from above it also lands on the lodge deck, which the roof holds in the
; key's shadow all day. That is what took the hall out of being a black box cut
; into a bright field (D-169's measurements are in the record).
;
; **(0.84, 0.82, 1.0) at 0.68.** Mostly white, with rather more blue than red and
; a shade more red than green, which is the "purple blue ish" of the ask rather
; than a cyan one: a violet cast subtracts from the peat's orange where a cyan
; one would subtract from its red and turn it grey. 0.68 is 30% of the key's
; 2.25, and both numbers were found together by rendering: at 0.55 against a 2.1
; key the picture was right and the ground had lost three points of median, so
; the pair came up until the top-down render put the open peat back over D-135's
; 58 of 255 (it reads 59.8). What the colour bought at the same time, measured
; on a body 15 m down the middle lane: red and blue went 114/31 to 145/51 — a
; third more light on it and half the cast off it, which is the difference
; between a target that reads as amber and one that reads as lit. It is a fill
; and not a second sun because the key is more than three times it and casts
; every shadow on the map; this one casts none and has `light_specular = 0`, so
; it adds no second highlight to anything.
;
; **`sky_mode = 1` (LIGHT_ONLY) is not optional.** `range_sky.gdshader` follows
; LIGHT0 for the sun disc and the glory (`sun_follow_light`), and a second light
; that reaches the sky can take LIGHT0 — the disc would jump to the
; south-south-west and disagree with every shadow on the map. D-061 records the
; same trap. It matters more now than it did: a fill above the horizon is a far
; more plausible LIGHT0 than one below it was.
;
; Serialised ROW-major like every `.tscn` `Transform3D`, and for once that is
; checked rather than asserted: `range_map._check_sun_and_sky` prints this
; node's elevation and bearing out of the built scene beside the Sun's, because
; the transpose bug that put the Sun 59.6 degrees from where it was meant to be
; is exactly the bug a hand-written basis invites. The light's own -Z — where
; the light *goes* — is (+0.457, -0.407, -0.791), so it comes from
; (-0.457, +0.407, +0.791): 24.0 degrees up on a bearing 210.0 degrees east of
; north.
[node name="Clouds" type="DirectionalLight3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(0.866025, 0.203369, -0.456773, 0, 0.913564, 0.406737, 0.5, -0.352244, 0.791175, 0, 30, 0)
light_color = Color(0.84, 0.82, 1, 1)
light_energy = 0.68
light_specular = 0.0
shadow_enabled = false
sky_mode = 1

[node name="Spawns" type="Node3D" parent="."]
